    'subject': 'Literature',
    'chapterTitle': 'Introduction to Literature and Its Genres',
    'body': 'Literature may be defined as the body of written or oral works that use language artistically to express ideas, emotions, experiences and imaginative visions of life, distinguishing itself from ordinary factual or technical writing by its deliberate use of style, form and creative expression to entertain, instruct, provoke thought and reflect the human condition. Literature is not merely a record of information; it is a crafted work of art that appeals to the intellect, the emotions and the imagination of the reader or audience simultaneously, and it often carries deeper meanings beyond the surface narrative, inviting interpretation, analysis and personal reflection.\n\nLiterature is generally studied in relation to its social function, since every literary work emerges from and reflects, to some degree, the society, culture, historical period and worldview of the writer who produced it. Literature performs several important functions in society: it entertains readers through engaging stories, characters and language; it educates and informs readers about historical events, social conditions, moral values and human experiences; it preserves the culture, traditions, beliefs and history of a people, particularly important in oral literature; it criticises and comments on social, political and moral issues within a society, often serving as a vehicle for reform; and it provides an outlet for the writer own imaginative and emotional expression, allowing readers to experience perspectives and emotions beyond their own direct experience.\n\nLiterature is broadly classified into three major genres, namely prose, drama and poetry, each with its own distinctive form, structure and conventions, though all three genres share the common purpose of using language artistically to communicate meaning and evoke response. Prose is the genre of literature written in ordinary, continuous language, organised into sentences and paragraphs rather than in verse form, and it includes forms such as the novel (a long fictional narrative, typically dealing with complex plots, multiple characters and extended themes), the novella (a fictional narrative of intermediate length, shorter than a novel but longer than a short story), the short story (a brief fictional narrative, usually focused on a single incident, a small number of characters and a unified effect), and prose non-fiction such as essays, biographies and autobiographies. Drama is the genre of literature intended primarily for performance before a live audience, presented through dialogue and action performed by actors on a stage, and it includes forms such as tragedy (a serious drama dealing with the downfall of a protagonist, often due to a tragic flaw, evoking pity and fear in the audience) and comedy (a lighter form of drama intended primarily to amuse and entertain, typically ending happily). Poetry is the genre of literature characterised by the compressed, rhythmic and often figurative use of language, frequently organised into lines and stanzas, employing devices such as rhyme, rhythm, imagery and figures of speech to convey meaning and emotion with heightened intensity and economy of expression compared to prose.\n\nLiterature can also be classified according to its origin and transmission into oral literature, which is transmitted by word of mouth from generation to generation, characteristic of traditional African societies before widespread literacy, and includes forms such as folktales, myths, legends, proverbs, riddles, praise songs and oral poetry; and written literature, which is composed, recorded and transmitted in written form, allowing for more permanent preservation and wider, more standardised distribution. African literature in English, French and Portuguese, as well as literature written in indigenous African languages, has grown significantly since the colonial and post-colonial periods, addressing themes such as colonialism, cultural conflict, tradition versus modernity, corruption, identity and nation-building, with prominent writers including Chinua Achebe, Wole Soyinka, Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie and Ngugi wa Thiong o, among many others, contributing significantly to both African and world literature.\n\nSample Exam-Style Questions:\n1. Question: Define literature and state three functions it performs in society. Answer: Literature is the body of written or oral works that use language artistically to express ideas, emotions and imaginative visions of life. It functions to entertain readers through engaging narratives, to educate readers about historical and social conditions, and to preserve the culture and traditions of a people, particularly in oral literature.\n2. Question: State the three major genres of literature and briefly describe the distinguishing feature of each. Answer: The three major genres are prose, drama and poetry. Prose is written in ordinary continuous language organised into sentences and paragraphs. Drama is written primarily for performance before an audience, presented through dialogue and action. Poetry is characterised by compressed, rhythmic and figurative language, often organised into lines and stanzas.\n3. Question: Distinguish between oral literature and written literature, giving one example of oral literature. Answer: Oral literature is transmitted by word of mouth from generation to generation, without being recorded in writing, an example being the folktale. Written literature is composed and recorded in written form, allowing for more permanent preservation and wider distribution across time and place.\n4. Question: Oyedele believes literature is only meant to entertain, while Toluwani believes it also serves to educate and criticise society. Evaluate both positions. Answer: Oyedele position captures only one function of literature. While entertainment is indeed an important function, Toluwani position is more comprehensive, since literature also educates readers about historical and social realities, preserves cultural traditions, and often critically comments on social, political and moral issues within a society, making it a powerful vehicle for both enjoyment and social reflection or reform.\n\nRevision and Summary Points:\n- Literature uses language artistically to express ideas, emotions and imaginative visions.\n- Literature is distinguished from ordinary factual writing by its stylistic and creative crafting.\n- Literature reflects the society and culture from which it emerges.\n- Literature entertains, educates, preserves culture, criticises society and expresses emotion.\n- The three major genres of literature are prose, drama and poetry.\n- Prose is written in ordinary continuous language, organised into sentences and paragraphs.\n- The novel is a long fictional prose narrative with complex plot and characters.\n- The novella is of intermediate length between a novel and a short story.\n- The short story is a brief narrative focused on a single incident or unified effect.\n- Drama is written primarily for performance before a live audience.\n- Tragedy deals with the downfall of a protagonist, evoking pity and fear.\n- Comedy is intended to amuse and typically ends happily.\n- Poetry uses compressed, rhythmic and figurative language.\n- Poetry often employs rhyme, rhythm, imagery and figures of speech.\n- Oral literature is transmitted by word of mouth across generations.\n- Oral literature includes folktales, myths, legends, proverbs and praise songs.\n- Written literature is composed and preserved in written form.\n- African literature addresses themes of colonialism and cultural conflict.\n- African literature also addresses tradition versus modernity and identity.\n- Chinua Achebe is a prominent African novelist.\n- Wole Soyinka is a prominent African dramatist and poet.\n- Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie is a prominent contemporary African novelist.\n- Ngugi wa Thiong o is a prominent African writer addressing colonial themes.\n- Literature can be studied in relation to its historical and social context.\n- A literary work often carries deeper meaning beyond its surface narrative.\n- Reading literature critically involves interpretation and personal reflection.\n- Literature invites emotional and intellectual engagement from its audience.\n- Studying literature develops empathy through exposure to other perspectives.\n- The study of genre helps readers understand a literary work structure and conventions.\n- Literature remains a vital means of cultural expression and social commentary worldwide.',
