  {
    'subject': 'Literature',
    'chapterTitle': 'Figures of Speech and Literary Devices in Literature',
    'body': 'Figures of speech, also referred to as literary or rhetorical devices, are deliberate departures from the ordinary, literal use of language, employed by writers across all three genres of literature, prose, drama and poetry, to achieve particular artistic, emotional or persuasive effects that plain, literal language alone could not achieve as powerfully or economically. A strong understanding of these devices, and the ability to identify them within a text and explain their specific effect, is essential for success in WAEC/WASSCE Literature, since examination questions frequently require candidates to identify a figure of speech used in a given passage and explain how it contributes to meaning, tone or characterisation.\n\nSimile is a figure of speech that makes an explicit comparison between two essentially different things, using the connecting words like or as, highlighting a particular quality or characteristic that the two things share, for example, his courage was as fierce as a lion. Metaphor is a figure of speech that makes an implicit or direct comparison between two essentially different things, without using like or as, asserting that one thing actually is the other in some symbolic sense, for example, Zetra was a rock during the crisis, implying strength and stability without literally comparing him to a rock using like or as. An extended metaphor sustains and develops a single metaphorical comparison across several lines, a whole stanza, or even an entire literary work, deepening and enriching its symbolic significance throughout.\n\nPersonification is a figure of speech that attributes distinctly human qualities, emotions, actions or characteristics to non-human entities, such as animals, objects, abstract ideas or natural phenomena, for example, the wind howled in anger through the trees. Symbolism is a literary device in which an object, character, action or event is used to represent a deeper, often abstract idea or meaning beyond its own literal significance within the narrative, for example, a caged bird within a story might symbolise a character loss of freedom or entrapment within restrictive social circumstances. Irony is a literary device involving a contrast or incongruity between what is expected or stated and what actually occurs or is true, and it takes several distinct forms: verbal irony occurs when a speaker says something that means the opposite of what they actually intend, often for sarcastic or humorous effect; situational irony occurs when the actual outcome of a situation is starkly different from, and often the opposite of, what was expected to happen; and dramatic irony, as discussed in the context of drama, occurs when the audience or reader possesses knowledge that one or more characters within the narrative lack.\n\nAllusion is a brief, often indirect reference within a literary work to a well-known person, place, event, or another work of literature, religion or mythology, relying on the reader own prior knowledge to fully understand and appreciate the deeper connection or meaning being suggested. Hyperbole is a figure of speech involving deliberate, obvious exaggeration, used not to deceive but to create emphasis, humour or strong emotional effect, for example, I have told you a million times to be careful. Understatement (litotes) is the opposite technique, in which a situation, quality or fact is deliberately presented as less significant or intense than it actually is, often for ironic or humorous effect. Euphemism is the substitution of a mild, indirect or less harsh expression in place of one that might be considered blunt, unpleasant or offensive, for example, describing someone as having passed away rather than directly stating that they died.\n\nApostrophe is a figure of speech in which the writer or speaker directly addresses an absent person, an inanimate object, or an abstract concept as though it were present and capable of understanding and responding. Paradox is a statement that, although it may initially appear self-contradictory or logically absurd on its surface, actually reveals a deeper underlying truth upon closer reflection and consideration. Oxymoron is a figure of speech that combines two normally contradictory or opposing terms directly together for striking rhetorical effect, such as the phrase deafening silence or bitter sweet. Understanding and correctly identifying these various figures of speech, along with clearly articulating their specific contribution to meaning, tone, characterisation or thematic development within a given passage, is a core, frequently examined skill within the WAEC/WASSCE Literature syllabus, applicable across all three genres of prose, drama and poetry.\n\nSample Exam-Style Questions:\n1. Question: Identify the figure of speech in the sentence Toluwani heart was a fortress, guarded against every disappointment, and explain its effect. Answer: This is a metaphor, since it directly compares Toluwani heart to a fortress without using like or as. The effect is to vividly convey Toluwani emotional self-protection and resistance to vulnerability, suggesting a deliberate, strong and impenetrable defence against being hurt.\n2. Question: Distinguish between verbal irony and situational irony, giving one example of each. Answer: Verbal irony occurs when a speaker says something that means the opposite of what they actually intend, for example saying what a beautiful day during a severe storm. Situational irony occurs when the actual outcome of events is starkly different from what was reasonably expected, for example a fire station burning down, since one would expect a fire station of all places to be well protected from fire.\n3. Question: Explain the difference between hyperbole and understatement, and state the purpose each serves. Answer: Hyperbole involves deliberate, obvious exaggeration used to create strong emphasis or emotional effect, such as saying I nearly died laughing. Understatement deliberately presents something as less significant than it actually is, often for ironic or humorous effect, such as describing a devastating storm merely as a bit windy. Both devices distort literal reality, but in opposite directions, to achieve particular rhetorical or emotional effects.\n4. Question: Oyedele reads a passage in which a character consistently refers to their poverty as their little financial inconvenience. Identify the figure of speech and explain its likely effect on the reader. Answer: This is an example of euphemism, since a harsh or unpleasant reality, severe poverty, is being replaced with a much milder, softer expression. The effect on the reader may be to reveal the character denial, pride, or attempt to minimise or cope with their difficult circumstances, and it may also create a subtly ironic or even poignant effect, depending on the surrounding context of the narrative.\n\nRevision and Summary Points:\n- Figures of speech are deliberate departures from literal language for artistic effect.\n- A simile compares two things using like or as.\n- A metaphor compares two things directly, without like or as.\n- An extended metaphor develops a single comparison across a larger portion of text.\n- Personification attributes human qualities to non-human things.\n- Symbolism uses an object or event to represent a deeper abstract meaning.\n- Irony involves a contrast between expectation and reality.\n- Verbal irony occurs when words mean the opposite of what is intended.\n- Situational irony occurs when an outcome differs sharply from expectation.\n- Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows more than certain characters.\n- Allusion is a brief reference to a well-known person, event or work.\n- Allusion relies on the reader prior knowledge for full effect.\n- Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration for emphasis or effect.\n- Understatement (litotes) presents something as less significant than it is.\n- Euphemism substitutes a mild expression for a harsh or blunt one.\n- Apostrophe directly addresses an absent or non-human entity as present.\n- Paradox appears self-contradictory but reveals a deeper truth.\n- Oxymoron combines two contradictory terms for striking effect.\n- Alliteration, assonance and onomatopoeia are sound-based literary devices.\n- Figures of speech appear across prose, drama and poetry alike.\n- Identifying figures of speech is a core skill tested in Literature examinations.\n- Explaining the effect of a device is as important as correctly naming it.\n- Figures of speech deepen meaning beyond literal interpretation.\n- Context determines which figure of speech is most appropriate to identify.\n- Multiple figures of speech may appear together within a single passage.\n- Skilled writers use these devices purposefully to shape reader response.\n- Careful, close reading is essential to accurately identify literary devices.\n- Figures of speech contribute to tone, mood, characterisation and theme.\n- Practice analysing varied texts builds confidence in identifying these devices.\n- Mastery of these devices supports strong performance in Literature examinations.',
